// item 130 Slice 3 — the blocking-tier (`go`) stream lowering.
// docs/design/130-stream-reactive-types.md §4.6 (the go row), §9.
//
// Three components, one per property the go tier owes:
//
//   Consumer — the CORE GUARANTEE (§0, exit test §10.2): a sync resource, then
//     a subscription bracket. Unloading the owner runs the inverses LIFO, so
//     `close` runs before the pool's inverse and no listener outlives the owner.
//   Parked   — a blocking `next` (§4.6): the activation occupies its goroutine
//     in the cancel-channel select until the provider emits, then carries on.
//     Faulting the provider instead terminates that park as `Faulted`, fails the
//     activation, and reverts the prefix — closing the subscription (§9 Part B).
//   Fanin    — `merge` (§1): two sources fanned into one DERIVED stream owned by
//     the subscription. Multi-source teardown rides the one bracket the
//     subscribe registers: `close` closes the merge, which detaches it from both
//     sources, and each source is left to its own bracket.
//   Chain    — the Slice 2 derived-stream combinator chain (`map`/`filter`/
//     `take`, §1). Each link is a DERIVED stream owned by the subscription, so
//     the whole chain unwinds off the ONE bracket the `subscribe` registers. A
//     filtered item never reaches the body, a mapped one reaches it transformed,
//     and a spent `take(n)` synthesises the `Closed` terminal that ends the loop
//     — the same three answers the py reference gives for the same source.
//   Windowed — the Slice 2 `block` drain WINDOW (§8): a `policy block drain <t>`
//     subscription resumes its provider on the CLOCK COEFFECT rather than at the
//     `next` that makes room. It is what pulls this tier's timer scheduler into
//     the emitted module, so the window fires on `RevlClockAdvance` — a step in
//     the timeline, the same answer the py reference gives — and the bracket
//     inverse cancels an armed one, leaving no orphaned schedule.
//   Iterate  — `every o in sub { … }` (Slice 4, §4.7): the async-iteration form,
//     a plain blocking `next` loop on this tier. Each delivered item runs the
//     effectful body (an emission to a required Sink); a `Closed` terminal ends
//     the loop without entering the body; unloading the owner while parked trips
//     the cancel channel, resolves the park as `Closed`, and closes the bracket.

component Consumer {
  let pool = effect Pool.open("orders", 2) undo pool.close()
  let src = effect Stream.source() undo src.close()
  let sub = subscribe src undo sub.close()
}

component Parked {
  let src = effect Stream.source() undo src.close()
  let sub = subscribe src undo sub.close()
  await sub.next()
}

component Fanin {
  let a = effect Stream.source() undo a.close()
  let b = effect Stream.source() undo b.close()
  let sub = subscribe merge(a, b) undo sub.close()
  await sub.next()
}

component Windowed {
  let src = effect Stream.source() undo src.close()
  let sub = subscribe src policy block buffer 2 drain 10ms undo sub.close()
  await sub.next()
}

service Sink { emission fn write(v: Str) }

component Iterate requires sink: Sink {
  let src = effect Stream.source() undo src.close()
  let sub = subscribe src undo sub.close()
  every o in sub {
    emit sink.write(o)
  }
}

component Chain requires sink: Sink {
  let src = effect Stream.source() undo src.close()
  let sub = subscribe src.filter(x => x != "skip").map(x => x + "!").take(2)
              undo sub.close()
  every o in sub {
    emit sink.write(o)
  }
}
